WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.880
On a rocky shore at Millport in Scotland, one barnacle lives high on the rock.

2
00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.140
Another covers the rock below.

3
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.060
Joseph Connell removed the lower one, Balanus, from around the upper one,

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.380
Chthamalus. Then Chthamalus survived well in the zone it normally lacks.

5
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.280
What had held it back?

6
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.000
Connell saw Balanus smother, undercut and crush its neighbour.

7
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.350
That is competition.

8
00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:34.750
Individuals use a resource in short supply, so each lowers the survival, growth or

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:35.910
reproduction of the others.

10
00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.650
The Lotka-Volterra model asks when two competitors can live together.

11
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:47.310
Each species is slowed by its own numbers, up to its carrying capacity, big K. It is

12
00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.710
also slowed by the other species, counted in its own units.

13
00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:56.150
α₁₂ is the effect of one species 2 individual on species 1.

14
00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:02.930
If α₁₂ is one half, two species 2 individuals crowd species 1 as much as one of its

15
00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:03.930
own.

16
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:06.363
Take values invented for practice.

17
00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:11.003
Big K is 100 for species 1, and 80 for species 2.

18
00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:13.043
Both alphas are 0.5.

19
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:17.703
With 20 of each, species 1 grows by 7 individuals per day.

20
00:01:18.563 --> 00:01:22.063
Now draw each isocline, the line where one species stops growing.

21
00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:27.883
Species 1 stops on a line from 100 on its own axis to 200 on the other.

22
00:01:28.803 --> 00:01:31.603
Species 2 stops on a line from 80 to 160.

23
00:01:32.303 --> 00:01:33.943
They cross at 80 and 40.

24
00:01:35.017 --> 00:01:36.417
Is that crossing stable?

25
00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:38.637
Let each species start rare.

26
00:01:39.737 --> 00:01:44.957
Species 1, rare among species 2 at its carrying capacity, grows by 0.3 per day.

27
00:01:45.897 --> 00:01:51.177
Species 2, rare among species 1 at 100, grows by 0.094 per day.

28
00:01:52.057 --> 00:01:53.217
Each can increase when rare.

29
00:01:54.037 --> 00:01:58.157
So in this sketch, the model predicts stable coexistence at 80 and 40.

30
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.480
Now change one coefficient.

31
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:09.280
Raise α₂₁ to 1. Species 2, rare among species 1 at 100, now has a growth rate of

32
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.340
−0.0625 per day. It cannot get started, while species 1 still can.

33
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.180
The model predicts that species 1 excludes species 2.

34
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:23.850
Both outcomes came from two conditions.

35
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.710
Species 1 needs K₁ above α₁₂K₂.

36
00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:31.650
Species 2 needs K₂ above α₂₁K₁.

37
00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.870
The growth rate never appears in them, so in this model the fastest grower need not

38
00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:42.870
win. Keep the rule: in this model, competitors coexist when each limits itself more

39
00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:43.850
than it limits the other.
